Migration runbook
=================

Alembic owns the schema: the application never calls create_all. Run every
command from the api/ directory. The database URL comes from DIRECT_DATABASE_URL,
falling back to DATABASE_URL (see alembic/env.py); it is never written in
alembic.ini.

Apply
-----

    alembic upgrade head              # what entrypoint.sh runs at start
    alembic upgrade head --sql        # print the SQL instead of running it
    alembic current                   # revision the database is at
    alembic history --verbose         # the chain, newest first

Migrations run in one transaction where the database supports transactional DDL
(PostgreSQL does), so a failed migration leaves the schema as it was.

Create a revision
-----------------

    alembic revision --autogenerate --rev-id 0003_add_widget -m "add widget"

The id is the sequence number plus a slug, at most 32 characters, and is also the
file name (alembic.ini: file_template). Review the draft before committing it:
renames look like a drop plus a create, and a missing downgrade is a defect. The
header and imports come out formatted; run your formatter on the operations, which
Alembic writes with single quotes and long lines.

Every downgrade must keep the data it removes:

- A dropped table goes through preserve_and_drop_table. Autogenerate writes that
  call for you.
- A dropped column goes through preserve_and_drop_column, which also needs the
  key columns that identify a row.
- op.drop_table and op.drop_column inside downgrade() fail
  tests/integration/test_migrations.py.

Keep literal column lengths in a migration, not imports from app.domain.constants:
a migration is a snapshot of the schema at its revision.

Roll back
---------

Newest first, one revision at a time:

    alembic downgrade -1
    alembic downgrade 0001_create_items    # newer revisions undone, this one stays

Dropped tables are copied aside before they go. On PostgreSQL the copy lives in the
migration_backup schema, named <table>__rollback_<UTC time to the microsecond>; on
SQLite it sits next to the table under the same name. The log records it as
migration.preserved with table= and backup=. The migration role needs the CREATE
privilege on the database to create that schema.

The copy carries rows only, no constraints or indexes, and PostgreSQL grants the
schema to nobody but its creator, so the Supabase Data API and the application role
cannot read it. Keep migration_backup out of the exposed schemas.

Restore from a backup
---------------------

Upgrade again so the table exists, then copy the rows back. List the columns when
the migrations changed them since the backup:

    alembic upgrade head
    psql "$DIRECT_DATABASE_URL" -c \
      "INSERT INTO items SELECT * FROM migration_backup.items__rollback_20261121101530123456"

Clean up
--------

Drop a backup once the restored data is verified, and the schema when it is empty:

    psql "$DIRECT_DATABASE_URL" -c \
      "DROP TABLE migration_backup.items__rollback_20261121101530123456"
    psql "$DIRECT_DATABASE_URL" -c "DROP SCHEMA migration_backup"

Validate before a deploy
------------------------

    alembic upgrade head --sql        # read what will run
    alembic check                     # models and migrations agree
    pytest tests/integration/test_migrations.py

Then rehearse on a copy of production: alembic upgrade head, alembic downgrade
base, alembic upgrade head. SQLite cannot prove PostgreSQL-only behaviour.
